{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"business-turnaround-track","kind":"workflow","title":"Turn around a declining business","description":"Takes a declining business through a turnaround in gated steps - cash triage, diagnosis, cost and focus decisions, a turnaround plan and weekly tracking.","category":"business-strategy","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["review","plan","operate"],"role":["founder","executive","consultant"],"advice_risk":["financial","legal"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["dataset","text","notes"],"output":["report","plan","table"],"risk":"read-only","invocation":"user","effort":"deep","interaction":"interactive","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"expert","tags":["turnaround","cash-crisis","restructuring","thirteen-week-cash-flow","recovery-plan"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["forecast-cash-flow","decide-whether-to-close-site","prioritize-strategic-initiatives","find-pricing-leakage"],"personas":["management-consultant","small-business-advisor"]},"args":[{"name":"business","description":"The business - what it sells, size, how results have declined and over what period, what has been tried, and the owners' goals.","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"figures","description":"Recent monthly sales, gross margin, overheads, cash balance, debts and payment terms, overdue creditors and any lender covenants. Step 1 asks for what is missing.","type":"text"}],"steps":[{"id":"cash","file":"steps/01-cash-triage.md","stage":"review","gate":"approve","artifact":"turnaround/01-cash-triage.md"},{"id":"diagnosis","file":"steps/02-diagnosis.md","stage":"review","gate":"approve","artifact":"turnaround/02-diagnosis.md"},{"id":"decisions","file":"steps/03-decisions.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"turnaround/03-decisions.md"},{"id":"plan","file":"steps/04-plan.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"turnaround/04-plan.md"},{"id":"tracking","file":"steps/05-weekly-tracking.md","stage":"operate","gate":"none","artifact":"turnaround/05-weekly-tracking.md"}],"output_contract":{"format":"markdown","sections":["Step artifact","Open questions"]},"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"Guides a business in decline through a turnaround: secure cash first, find the real causes, make the hard choices, write a plan and track it weekly. Each step writes one artifact and stops for approval.\n\n<business>\n{{business}}\n</business>\n{{#figures}}\n\n<figures>\n{{figures}}\n</figures>\n{{/figures}}\n\nRules for every step:\n- You give general information, not professional advice. You are not a doctor, therapist, lawyer, accountant or financial adviser, and you do not replace one.\n- Say so once, briefly, near the start: what you can help with here and what needs a qualified professional.\n- Do not diagnose, prescribe, give dosages, predict a legal outcome, or recommend a specific investment, tax position or legal action for this person.\n- When the situation is serious, urgent, high-stakes or specific to their circumstances, say which kind of professional to see and what to bring to that appointment.\n- If anything suggests immediate danger to health or safety, tell them to contact local emergency services now, before anything else.\n- Rules, prices and laws differ by country and change over time. Name the assumption you are making and tell them to check it locally.\n- Cash comes before profit: no step may propose spending that the cash forecast cannot carry.\n- Use only figures given or confirmed; mark gaps as [X] and show arithmetic.\n- If the business may be unable to pay its debts as they fall due, say so plainly and tell the owner to see an insolvency practitioner or restructuring adviser now; directors' duties change in that situation and vary by country.\n- Staff cuts, contract exits and creditor deals need legal advice and must follow local law; never draft anything that misleads staff, creditors or lenders.","steps":[{"id":"cash","file":"steps/01-cash-triage.md","stage":"review","gate":"approve","artifact":"turnaround/01-cash-triage.md","text":"# Step 1: Cash triage\n\n1. Build a 13-week cash forecast: opening cash, receipts, payments, closing cash by week. Show the lowest point and when it hits.\n2. Quick cash levers ranked by speed and harm: chase overdue debtors, sell surplus stock, pause non-essential spend, agree payment plans with key creditors or tax authorities, defer capital spend.\n3. Solvency check: can debts be paid as they fall due? If not, or if close, say the owner must take insolvency advice this week.\n4. Immediate controls: who approves every payment, daily cash check.\n\nSections: 13-week forecast, Cash levers, Solvency check, Controls, Open questions.\n\nStop and wait for approval."},{"id":"diagnosis","file":"steps/02-diagnosis.md","stage":"review","gate":"approve","artifact":"turnaround/02-diagnosis.md","text":"# Step 2: Diagnosis\n\n1. Trace the decline: sales by product, customer and channel; gross margin; overheads, over 2 to 3 years where figures allow.\n2. Separate external causes (market, competitor, costs) from internal ones (pricing, product, operations, management, debt).\n3. Find which products, customers, sites or channels make or lose money after their own costs.\n4. Name the core of the business worth saving, and say honestly if there may be none.\n\nSections: Decline in numbers, Causes, Profit by segment, Viable core, Open questions.\n\nStop and wait for approval."},{"id":"decisions","file":"steps/03-decisions.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"turnaround/03-decisions.md","text":"# Step 3: Decisions\n\n1. Focus: what to keep, fix, shrink or exit, from the profit-by-segment view.\n2. Cost: reductions sized and timed, separating cuts that hurt the viable core from those that do not. Flag staff and contract changes for legal advice.\n3. Revenue: pricing, mix and customer actions with quick payback.\n4. Funding: whether the plan needs new money or lender agreement, and the options to discuss with advisers.\n5. Show the effect of each decision on the 13-week cash forecast and on monthly profit.\n\nSections: Focus decisions, Cost actions, Revenue actions, Funding, Effect on cash and profit, Open questions.\n\nStop and wait for approval."},{"id":"plan","file":"steps/04-plan.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"turnaround/04-plan.md","text":"# Step 4: Turnaround plan\n\n1. A 100-day plan: actions with owner, date and cash effect, in order.\n2. Milestones at day 30, 60 and 100, and the trigger that means the plan is failing.\n3. Stakeholder communication: staff, key customers, suppliers, lender. Honest and agreed with advisers.\n4. Governance: a weekly turnaround meeting and who attends.\n\nSections: 100-day plan, Milestones and triggers, Stakeholder communication, Governance, Open questions.\n\nStop and wait for approval."},{"id":"tracking","file":"steps/05-weekly-tracking.md","stage":"operate","gate":"none","artifact":"turnaround/05-weekly-tracking.md","text":"# Step 5: Weekly tracking\n\n1. A one-page weekly dashboard: cash actual versus forecast, sales, gross margin, overdue debtors and creditors, action status.\n2. A rolling 13-week forecast update routine.\n3. Rules for escalation when a trigger is hit.\n4. Fill it in with the latest figures the owner supplies, and comment on variances.\n\nSections: Dashboard, Forecast routine, Escalation rules, This week's commentary."}]}
